.TH TRIM-CORRUPT 1 "2026-10-10" "trim_corrupt.sh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trim_corrupt.sh \- trim .ts files whose audio runs past the video end
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trim_corrupt.sh
[\fIINPUT\fP]
[\fIOUTPUT_DIR\fP]
[\fIPARALLEL\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trim_corrupt.sh
fixes MPEG transport stream (
.B .ts
) recordings where the audio track extends more than
.B 5 seconds
past the end of the video, by trimming the file to the end of the video
without re-encoding.
.P
For each
.B .ts
file, the end timestamp of the last video packet of the first video stream
and the last audio packet of the first audio stream
are read with
.BR ffprobe (1):
.IP \(bu 2
video end:
.B last pts_time
of the first video stream (by codec type).
.IP \(bu 2
audio end:
.B last pts_time
of the first audio stream (by codec type).
.P
If both timestamps can be read and the audio end is more than 5 seconds after
the video end, the file is trimmed to the video end and the original is
deleted. Files whose audio and video are aligned within 5 seconds are left
untouched. If the timestamps cannot be read, the file is skipped.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I INPUT
A single
.B .ts
file, or a directory of
.B .ts
files. Defaults to the current directory.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If a
.B file
is given, only that file is processed.
.IP \(bu 2
If a
.B directory
is given, all
.B *.ts
files in it are processed (top level only, not recursive).
.IP \(bu 2
Anything else prints an error and the script exits with status 1.
.RE
.TP
.I OUTPUT_DIR
Directory where trimmed copies are written. Defaults to
.BR ./trimmed .
Created if it does not exist.
.TP
.I PARALLEL
Maximum concurrent files. Defaults to
.BR nproc .
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.IP \(bu 2
Trimming is performed with
.BR ffmpeg (1)
as
.BR "ffmpeg \-v error \-i FILE \-t VID_END \-c copy OUT" ,
a stream copy (no re-encode), cut at the video end timestamp.
.IP \(bu 2
The original file is deleted
.B only
if the trimmed output exists and is non-empty; otherwise a warning is
printed and the original is kept.
.IP \(bu 2
The 5-second threshold and the trim length are computed with floating point,
so sub-second differences are handled.
.IP \(bu 2
Per-file messages report the trim point, the audio end, and the difference;
aligned files report
.B OK: streams roughly aligned.
.SH EXAMPLES
Process all
.B .ts
files in the current directory into
.BR ./trimmed :
.IP
.EX
trim_corrupt.sh
.EE
.P
Process a specific recording:
.IP
.EX
trim_corrupt.sh ~/Videos/news.ts
.EE
.P
Process a directory, writing into a named output folder:
.IP
.EX
trim_corrupt.sh ~/Videos/recordings ~/Videos/fixed
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Completed normally.
.TP
.B 1
INPUT is neither a file nor a directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1)
